{
    "map": {
        "cellsize.descr": "The pixel size that will be used for the new raster dataset.",
        "cellsize.title": "Cellsize",
        "coordinate_system_for_the_raster.descr": "The coordinate system for the output raster dataset.",
        "coordinate_system_for_the_raster.title": "Spatial Reference for  Raster",
        "description": "Merges multiple raster datasets into a new raster dataset.",
        "input_rasters.descr": "The raster datasets that you want to merge together. The inputs must have the same number of bands and same bit depth.",
        "input_rasters.title": "Input Rasters",
        "mosaic_colormap_mode.descr": "<xdoc><p>Applies when the input raster datasets have a colormap.</p><p>Specifies the method that will be used to choose which color map from the input rasters will be applied to the mosaic output.</p><ul><li><span title=\"FIRST\">First</span>—The color map from the first raster dataset in the list will be applied to the output raster mosaic. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"LAST\">Last</span>—The color map from the last raster dataset in the list will be applied to the output raster mosaic.</li><li><span title=\"MATCH\">Match</span>—All the color maps will be considered when mosaicking. If all possible values are already used (for the bit depth), the tool will match the value with the closest available color.</li><li><span title=\"REJECT\">Reject</span>—Only the raster datasets that do not have a color map associated with them will be mosaicked.</li></ul><p>For more information about each colormap mode, see the Mosaic colormap mode help topic.</p></xdoc>",
        "mosaic_colormap_mode.domain.FIRST": "First",
        "mosaic_colormap_mode.domain.LAST": "Last",
        "mosaic_colormap_mode.domain.MATCH": "Match",
        "mosaic_colormap_mode.domain.REJECT": "Reject",
        "mosaic_colormap_mode.title": "Mosaic Colormap Mode",
        "mosaic_method.descr": "<xdoc><p>The method used to mosaic overlapping areas.</p><ul><li><span title=\"FIRST\">First</span>—The output cell value of the overlapping areas will be the value from the first raster dataset mosaicked into that location.</li><li><span title=\"LAST\">Last</span>—The output cell value of the overlapping areas will be the value from the last raster dataset mosaicked into that location. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"BLEND\">Blend</span>—The output cell value of the overlapping areas will be a horizontally weighted calculation of the values of the cells in the overlapping area.</li><li><span title=\"MEAN\">Mean</span>—The output cell value of the overlapping areas will be the average value of the overlapping cells.</li><li><span title=\"MINIMUM\">Minimum</span>—The output cell value of the overlapping areas will be the minimum value of the overlapping cells.</li><li><span title=\"MAXIMUM\">Maximum</span>—The output cell value of the overlapping areas will be the maximum value of the overlapping cells.</li><li><span title=\"SUM\">Sum</span>—The output cell value of the overlapping areas will be the total sum of the overlapping cells.</li></ul><p>For more information about each mosaic operator, refer to the Mosaic Operator help topic.</p></xdoc>",
        "mosaic_method.domain.BLEND": "Blend",
        "mosaic_method.domain.FIRST": "First",
        "mosaic_method.domain.LAST": "Last",
        "mosaic_method.domain.MAXIMUM": "Maximum",
        "mosaic_method.domain.MEAN": "Mean",
        "mosaic_method.domain.MINIMUM": "Minimum",
        "mosaic_method.domain.SUM": "Sum",
        "mosaic_method.title": "Mosaic Operator",
        "number_of_bands.descr": "The number of bands that the output raster will have.",
        "number_of_bands.title": "Number of Bands",
        "output_location.descr": "The folder or geodatabase to store the raster.",
        "output_location.title": "Output Location",
        "output_raster_dataset.title": "Output Raster Dataset",
        "pixel_type.descr": "<xdoc><p>The bit depth, or radiometric resolution of the mosaic dataset.</p><p>If you do not set the pixel type, the 8-bit default will be used and your output may be incorrect.</p><ul><li><span title=\"1_BIT\">1 bit</span>—The pixel type will be a 1-bit unsigned integer. The values can be 0 or 1.</li><li><span title=\"2_BIT\">2 bit</span>—The pixel type will be a 2-bit unsigned integer. The values supported can range from 0 to 3.</li><li><span title=\"4_BIT\">4 bit</span>—The pixel type will be a 4-bit unsigned integer. The values supported can range from 0 to 15.</li><li><span title=\"8_BIT_UNSIGNED\">8 bit unsigned</span>—The pixel type will be an unsigned 8-bit data type. The values supported can range from 0 to 255.</li><li><span title=\"8_BIT_SIGNED\">8 bit signed</span>—The pixel type will be a signed 8-bit data type. The values supported can range from -128 to 127.</li><li><span title=\"16_BIT_UNSIGNED\">16 bit unsigned</span>—The pixel type will be a 16-bit unsigned data type. The values can range from 0 to 65,535.</li><li><span title=\"16_BIT_SIGNED\">16 bit signed</span>—The pixel type will be a 16-bit signed data type. The values can range from -32,768 to 32,767.</li><li><span title=\"32_BIT_UNSIGNED\">32 bit unsigned</span>—The pixel type will be a 32-bit unsigned data type. The values can range from 0 to 4,294,967,295.</li><li><span title=\"32_BIT_SIGNED\">32 bit signed</span>—The pixel type will be a 32-bit signed data type. The values can range from -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647.</li><li><span title=\"32_BIT_FLOAT\">32 bit float</span>—The pixel type will be a 32-bit data type supporting decimals.</li><li><span title=\"64_BIT\">64 bit</span>—The pixel type will be a 64-bit data type supporting decimals.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "pixel_type.domain.16_BIT_SIGNED": "16 bit signed",
        "pixel_type.domain.16_BIT_UNSIGNED": "16 bit unsigned",
        "pixel_type.domain.1_BIT": "1 bit",
        "pixel_type.domain.2_BIT": "2 bit",
        "pixel_type.domain.32_BIT_FLOAT": "32 bit float",
        "pixel_type.domain.32_BIT_SIGNED": "32 bit signed",
        "pixel_type.domain.32_BIT_UNSIGNED": "32 bit unsigned",
        "pixel_type.domain.4_BIT": "4 bit",
        "pixel_type.domain.64_BIT": "64 bit",
        "pixel_type.domain.8_BIT_SIGNED": "8 bit signed",
        "pixel_type.domain.8_BIT_UNSIGNED": "8 bit unsigned",
        "pixel_type.title": "Pixel Type",
        "raster_dataset_name_with_extension.descr": "<xdoc><p>The name of the dataset you are creating.</p><p>When storing the raster dataset in a file format, specify the file extension as follows:</p><ul><li>.bil—Esri BIL</li><li>.bip—Esri BIP</li><li>.bmp—BMP</li><li>.bsq—Esri BSQ</li><li>.dat—ENVI DAT</li><li>.gif—GIF</li><li>.img—ERDAS IMAGINE</li><li>.jpg—JPEG</li><li>.jp2—JPEG 2000</li><li>.png—PNG</li><li>.tif—TIFF</li><li>No extension for Esri Grid</li></ul><p>When storing a raster dataset in a geodatabase, do not add a file extension to the name of the raster dataset.</p><p>When storing a raster dataset to a JPEG format file, a JPEG 2000 format file, a TIFF format file, or a geodatabase, you can specify <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Compression Type</span> and <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Compression Quality</span> values in the geoprocessing environments.</p></xdoc>",
        "raster_dataset_name_with_extension.title": "Raster Dataset Name with Extension",
        "title": "Mosaic To New Raster"
    }
}
